title: Does the designer’s self belong in a design?
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

I think we should care about designers. who they are. who built an idea of a previous one. The issue of design is often an intellectual property one, we often pretened NOT to be referncing things so as to not draw a problematic connection for legal reasons. And then individual designers are often subsumed by the coporations that they work for so a design is from a company, not an individual.

The few cases that is not ture: [[Dieter Rams]] for [[Braun]]; though he WAS Braun's design leader... [[Jony Ive]] for [[Apple]], same as Rams... Ives has a more problematic space to live in now that he left Apple and is doing his own studio for others (the new Ferarri looks like an apple magic mouse, does ive not have a different visual language???)

There are designers like [[Philippe Starck]], [[Karim Rashid]], [[Michael Graves]], who did actually become household names to some for a short while, thanks to their work with [[Target]] and similar... [[Ikea]] still always documents the designers for each product when an external designer or studio does the work. 

However contempirary design at say Target is now named in connection with inlfuencers and TV personalities more than an actual known designer. They aren't "designing" the things (like the Magnolia collections from the GAines') as much as just helping select the qualities and aesthetics of objects...

Room and board doesn't care as much about telling you who designed their things as say all the older furniture companies based on modernist design legends, those names earned cultural relevance, do contemporary ones? Will contemporary ones? Are corporations too important now?